{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"14644ff366b0e4a2fe7904856b50e839143ec3093386dee3d5434bbfa49ea07e","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_12c45596ff8feb11309e214acb817ab6a919765577620574f5dbcf76f95477b8","overall_assessment":"The paper makes a strong contribution to the field of document representation learning by introducing Doc2VecC, a method that combines the efficiency of Word2Vec with a novel corruption-based regularization. The theoretical analysis providing a clear interpretation of the corruption as data-dependent regularization is a significant strength. The experimental results demonstrate that the method achieves competitive accuracy with substantially improved training and testing efficiency compared to state-of-the-art alternatives. The work effectively addresses the scalability challenges of existing document vector models and provides valuable insights into the role of regularization in learning compositional document representations.","paper_id":"B1Igu2ogg","paper_identity_sha256":"29d3de15578a900a91a6c32a39fb05eac674372d0b559216db44e0a1f8edb4fb","prompt_identity_sha256":"65f18a6b274ed031c9662bf95a403125b4b1b5d55d0a3db8378a449bc6b56bf2","raw_response_sha256":"6be7eb2c2267528e94dbfa4d9092cdef30191e6696950d39d3d4cce94db244ca","request_identity_sha256":"e732fe5032779254b6b2160234338e3002a30fa16f0156ece2d7e08f84ce9ce4","reviewer_identity_sha256":"6832030d37bc6b05f4de12f913ecdea182594ad5d7ac3dcd33edcb54d7bd3a1a","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v2","structural_context_sha256":"ec8cc86da9d81ce8a38b5be410cca5062f602d489d08646f9fd3ed8657b0909d","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-003","SRC-004","SRC-005"],"review_text":"The paper addresses the established problem of learning efficient document vector representations. While it does not introduce a novel task or dataset, it formulates a specific challenge: how to maintain the semantic quality of simple average-based document representations (which are computationally efficient) while ensuring they capture global document semantics during the learning phase. This formulation is motivated by the limitations of Paragraph Vectors, specifically the high parameter complexity and expensive inference for unseen documents. The problem setup is well-motivated by the need for scalability and test-time efficiency, distinguishing it from prior work that often relies on per-document parameters or complex post-processing.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-PROB"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-011","SRC-012","SRC-013","SRC-014"],"review_text":"The proposed method, Doc2VecC, introduces a novel architectural modification to the Word2Vec framework. Instead of learning a unique vector for each document (as in Paragraph Vectors), it represents the document as the average of word embeddings. The key algorithmic novelty lies in the 'corruption' mechanism, where words are randomly dropped during training with an unbiased scaling factor. This is not merely a hyperparameter tweak but a structural change that alters the optimization landscape. The method effectively decouples model complexity from corpus size, relying only on vocabulary size, which is a significant architectural advance for scalability. The use of negative sampling to approximate the probability is standard, but the integration of the corrupted global context into the CBoW objective is a distinct methodological contribution.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-MTH"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-015","SRC-016","SRC-017","SRC-018","SRC-019","SRC-020"],"review_text":"The paper provides a rigorous theoretical analysis demonstrating that the corruption mechanism acts as a data-dependent regularization. By performing a second-order Taylor expansion of the log-likelihood with respect to the corrupted input, the authors derive that the expected loss includes a regularization term proportional to the variance of the corruption. This analysis reveals that the method inherently suppresses the embeddings of frequent but uninformative words (like stop words) while preserving the signal from rare or informative words. This insight deepens the understanding of why simple averaging works when trained with corruption, linking it to the principles of denoising autoencoders and dropout regularization, but applied specifically to the document representation context. The derivation is clear and provides a strong justification for the method's design choices.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-ANL"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-021","SRC-023","SRC-027","SRC-028","SRC-032","SRC-033"],"review_text":"The experimental setup is comprehensive, evaluating the method on three distinct tasks: sentiment analysis (IMDB), document classification (Wikipedia), and semantic relatedness (SICK). The paper introduces a meaningful evaluation protocol that compares not only accuracy but also training and testing efficiency, which is a critical aspect of the proposed method's value proposition. The inclusion of a detailed comparison of training times and representation generation times (Table 2) adds value beyond standard accuracy benchmarks. Furthermore, the analysis of the learned embeddings (Table 3) provides qualitative evidence supporting the theoretical claims about regularization. The use of t-SNE visualizations for document clustering offers additional insight into the geometric properties of the learned representations.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-012","SRC-021"],"review_text":"Doc2VecC creatively combines the simplicity of Word2Vec's architecture with the regularization benefits of corruption (similar to dropout or denoising autoencoders). The rationale for this combination is well-articulated: the corruption allows the model to learn robust word embeddings that, when averaged, form a meaningful document vector without requiring per-document parameters. This synergy addresses the trade-off between model complexity and representation quality. The paper clearly distinguishes this approach from prior work by Huang et al. (2012), who also used average word embeddings but without the corruption mechanism, highlighting that the corruption is critical for both performance and speed. The combination is not superficial; it fundamentally changes how the global context is learned and regularized.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-COM"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-026","SRC-027","SRC-038","SRC-039"],"review_text":"The paper provides a critical assessment of existing methods, particularly Paragraph Vectors and Skip-thought vectors, by highlighting their computational inefficiencies. It explicitly points out that Paragraph Vectors requires expensive inference for unseen documents and that Skip-thought vectors are slow to train and test. The experiments demonstrate that Doc2VecC significantly outperforms these methods in efficiency while matching or exceeding their accuracy on certain tasks. Additionally, the paper acknowledges the limitations of Doc2VecC on shorter sentences (SICK dataset) compared to LSTM-based methods, providing a balanced view of the method's applicability. This critical evaluation of the trade-offs between efficiency and performance across different document lengths is a valuable contribution to the field.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-NEG"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"b6b6a286c83b05d6026a0ed64e6fc1f669d162e2f0e78e27e545bda4420bb504","paper_id":"B1Igu2ogg","paper_ordinal":7,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"6be7eb2c2267528e94dbfa4d9092cdef30191e6696950d39d3d4cce94db244ca","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"B1Igu2ogg","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"248140c064696e33ab478805f0c6be2852d7efe3680e9edc7bc748bcedd88a4f","request_identity_sha256":"e732fe5032779254b6b2160234338e3002a30fa16f0156ece2d7e08f84ce9ce4","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v2","usage":{"completion_tokens":1447,"prompt_tokens":19692,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":21139},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"e732fe5032779254b6b2160234338e3002a30fa16f0156ece2d7e08f84ce9ce4","role":"ORIG","role_ordinal":3,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v1"}